<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Isolated QA instance. Deliberately EMPTY.

	     Omitting the App Group is the isolation mechanism, not an oversight:
	     without the entitlement iOS refuses the QA build access to
	     group.com.youssefsafwat.mali, so it cannot read or write the real
	     app's shared capture container even though SharedCaptureStore still
	     names that group. The mechanism differs per API and neither reaches
	     real data: containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)
	     returns nil and is guarded (SharedCaptureStore.swift:605), while
	     UserDefaults(suiteName:) does NOT return nil — iOS silently detaches
	     it to this app's own sandbox. Shared-keychain reads fail with
	     errSecMissingEntitlement, which the code already treats as absent.
	     Capture is non-functional in QA; it is never cross-wired.

	     Omitting keychain-access-groups leaves flutter_secure_storage on the
	     default group — this bundle id — so the QA build gets its own
	     SQLCipher key and its own session, and cannot decrypt the real app's
	     database.

	     aps-environment and Sign in with Apple are omitted so automatic
	     signing can provision this App ID without portal capability setup.
	     QA authenticates with email/password; push is verified on the real
	     production build, not here. -->
</dict>
</plist>
